+From: OpenSSL commit 9e209944b3 by Richard Levitte (OpenSSL_1_1_1-stable, CVE-2023-2650), applied unchanged to OpenSSL 1.0.1u for android-kitkat-tls12
+Subject: cve-2023-2650
+
+OBJ_obj2txt() took O(n^2) time for a gigantic OID sub-identifier; OIDs in a
+peer certificate reach it before verification (NativeCrypto OID strings,
+X509_NAME printing). OIDs longer than 586 bytes are now rejected. Fixed in
+1.0.2zh and 1.1.1u (2023-05), after the last 1.0.1 release.
+---
+diff --git a/crypto/objects/obj_dat.c b/crypto/objects/obj_dat.c
+index aca382a..e67852c 100644
+--- a/crypto/objects/obj_dat.c
++++ b/crypto/objects/obj_dat.c
+@@ -516,6 +516,25 @@ int OBJ_obj2txt(char *buf, int buf_len, const ASN1_OBJECT *a, int no_name)
+     first = 1;
+     bl = NULL;
+ 
++    /*
++     * RFC 2578 (STD 58) says this about OBJECT IDENTIFIERs:
++     *
++     * > 3.5. OBJECT IDENTIFIER values
++     * >
++     * > An OBJECT IDENTIFIER value is an ordered list of non-negative
++     * > numbers. For the SMIv2, each number in the list is referred to as a
++     * > sub-identifier, there are at most 128 sub-identifiers in a value,
++     * > and each sub-identifier has a maximum value of 2^32-1 (4294967295
++     * > decimal).
++     *
++     * So a legitimate OID according to this RFC is at most (32 * 128 / 7),
++     * i.e. 586 bytes long.
++     *
++     * Ref: https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc2578#section-3.5
++     */
++    if (len > 586)
++        goto err;
++
+     while (len > 0) {
+         l = 0;
+         use_bn = 0;
